{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794389058,"nonce":"CVbtOFzW98E40qrl8GlgmNgcj00=","title":"A library header named like its Haxe class is shadowed on case-insensitive filesystems","message":"hxcpp generates `include/Counter.h` for a Haxe class `Counter` and puts\n`-Iinclude` first. On macOS and Windows `#include \"counter.h\"` from that\nheader then resolves to the generated header itself, so the library's C\ndeclarations are never seen and the build fails with every hxn type\nundeclared.\n\nSeen while writing tests/counter: the C header was `counter.h`, the Haxe\nclass `Counter`. Renaming the header to `counter_abi.h` fixed it.\n\nThe generator must not emit a header whose name differs from a generated\nHaxe class header only by case. A fixed suffix on the C header name, as the\nfixture now uses, avoids it for every library.","files":null}]}